boxed{...}.\n\nSuppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5cf7b8ca305c7d7a025959f056cbc91266a163b7c576ce4ace732a5d90287f98"},"seconds":69.75860142707825,"seed":686101727699564961,"stop":"eos","text":"Given that the roots of the polynomial \\(x^2 - mx + n\\) are positive prime integers, let's denote these roots by \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n\n\\[\nm = p + q \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n = pq\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(m < 20\\), we need to find all pairs of prime numbers \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) such that \\(p + q < 20\\). We will then determine the corresponding values of \\(n = pq\\).\n\nFirst, list all prime numbers less than 20:\n\\[2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19\\]\n\nNow, consider all pairs \\((p, q)\\) where \\(p \\leq q\\) and \\(p + q < 20\\):\n\n1. \\(p = 2\\):\n   - \\(q = 2\\): \\(p + q = 4 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 2 = 4\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\): \\(p + q = 5 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 3 = 6\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\): \\(p + q = 7 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 5 = 10\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\): \\(p + q = 9 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 7 = 14\\)\n   - \\(q = 11\\): \\(p + q = 13 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 11 = 22\\)\n   - \\(q = 13\\): \\(p + q = 15 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 13 = 26\\)\n   - \\(q = 17\\): \\(p + q = 19 < 20\\), \\(n = 2 \\times 17 = 34\\)\n\n2. \\(p = 3\\):\n   - \\(q = 3\\): \\(p + q = 6 < 20\\), \\(n = 3 \\times 3 = 9\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\): \\(p + q = 8 < 20\\), \\(n = 3 \\times 5 = 15\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\): \\(p + q = 10 < 20\\), \\(n = 3 \\times 7 = 21\\)\n   - \\(q = 11\\): \\(p + q = 14 < 20\\), \\(n = 3 \\times 11 = 33\\)\n   - \\(q = 13\\): \\(p + q = 16 < 20\\), \\(n = 3 \\times 13 = 39\\)\n\n3. \\(p = 5\\):\n   - \\(q = 5\\): \\(p + q = 10 < 20\\), \\(n = 5 \\times 5 = 25\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\): \\(p + q = 12 < 20\\), \\(n = 5 \\times 7 = 35\\)\n   - \\(q = 11\\): \\(p + q = 16 < 20\\), \\(n = 5 \\times 11 = 55\\)\n\n4. \\(p = 7\\):\n   - \\(q = 7\\): \\(p + q = 14 < 20\\), \\(n = 7 \\times 7 = 49\\)\n\n5. \\(p = 11\\):\n   - \\(q = 11\\): \\(p + q = 22 \\geq 20\\) (not valid)\n\n6. \\(p = 13\\):\n   - \\(q = 13\\): \\(p + q = 26 \\geq 20\\) (not valid)\n\n7. \\(p = 17\\):\n   - \\(q = 17\\): \\(p + q = 34 \\geq 20\\) (not valid)\n\n8. \\(p = 19\\):\n   - \\(q = 19\\): \\(p + q = 38 \\geq 20\\) (not valid)\n\nFrom the above, the possible values of \\(n\\) are:\n\\[4, 6, 9, 10, 14, 15, 21, 22, 25, 26, 33, 34, 35, 39, 49, 55\\]\n\nCounting these unique values, we have 16 possible values of \\(n\\).\n\nThus, the number of possible values of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{16}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
